# Normalise line endings so an editor cannot turn a small change into a
# whole-file diff.
#
# Why this exists: measured in this repository, a 20-line edit to media.py
# appeared as a 5360-line diff because the file had been rewritten with CRLF, and
# the same happened three times in one session. `text=auto` fixes that at the
# source -- git converts a working-tree file's endings to the canonical form
# before comparing, so a stray CRLF no longer shows up as churn, and the committed
# blob is always LF.
#
# `eol=lf` additionally makes checkout write LF, so the bytes on disk match on
# every platform. CI runs on Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that are CRLF in the repository ON PURPOSE, excluded from normalisation.
#
# This is not an oversight being preserved; it is a decision this file must not
# undo. Commit 9c26a66 ("restore the CRLF line endings two test files had before
# the rewrite") put these back after a mechanical rewrite normalised them, because
# the normalisation published 6855 lines of phantom churn that hid the ~181 lines
# the branch actually changed. `git add --renormalize` on this list produces
# 5591 insertions and 5591 deletions -- byte-identical apart from the newline
# bytes, and unreviewable.
#
# `-text` leaves the bytes alone. `diff` keeps them readable as text rather than
# letting git call them binary, which would hide their changes entirely -- the
# same failure by a different route.
custom_components/frigate_vision/strings.json -text diff
custom_components/frigate_vision/translations/en.json -text diff
custom_components/frigate_vision/translations/zh-Hans.json -text diff
tests/test_idempotency.py -text diff
tests/test_media.py -text diff

# Explicit binary markers. `text=auto` already detects these by content, but
# naming them keeps the intent visible and stops a later rule from mangling them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
